TCP vs UDP — Which Protocol Does What, and Why It Matters 🌐

TCP and UDP are two core transport protocols that move data across networks — but they behave very differently. Here’s a breakdown to help you understand when and why each one is used:

🟠 TCP – Transmission Control Protocol
Reliable and secure. TCP is connection-oriented, which means it ensures data is received in order and without errors.
Used in applications where accuracy matters — like web browsing, email, and file transfers.
🔹 20-byte header
🔹 Guarantees delivery and order
🔹 Includes flow control and retransmission

🔵 UDP – User Datagram Protocol
Fast but unreliable. UDP skips connection setup to reduce delay, making it ideal for real-time apps like video streaming and online games.
There’s no delivery guarantee, but it’s perfect when speed is more important than precision.
🔹 8-byte header
🔹 No flow control or packet ordering
🔹 Lightweight and faster

📡 Delivery Modes Matter Too:

Unicast: One-to-one (used by both)

Multicast & Broadcast: One-to-many (often UDP-based)

